1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Circuit breakers protect gate from a dead
billing dependency. The tide API is a
different beast: intermittent 503s, bursty
rate limits, and a vendor that hates
connection stampedes.

2
00:00:12,119 --> 00:00:23,183
Resilience4j gives you composable
decorators — retry, rate limiter,
bulkhead, time limiter, circuit breaker —
you can stack around that call without
reinventing thread pools in every service.

3
00:00:23,303 --> 00:00:38,304
Scheduling asks the tide service for
predicted height before confirming a
berth. Wrap the client: Look at this java
for a moment. Look at this example.
resilience4j: Read the composition as a
story.

4
00:00:38,423 --> 00:00:46,415
Rate limiter refuses to stampede the
vendor — when the bucket is empty, callers
fail in ~200ms instead of opening fifty
sockets.

5
00:00:46,536 --> 00:00:53,496
Bulkhead caps concurrent tide calls so
scheduling’s other threads keep working on
berth conflicts that do not need tide.

6
00:00:53,615 --> 00:01:05,495
Retry absorbs blips with bounded attempts
and backoff; TideStationUnknownException
is ignored so you do not thrash on
permanent 404s. Time limiter kills hung
calls at one second.

7
00:01:05,615 --> 00:01:17,231
Circuit breaker opens when the vendor is
truly down and routes to cachedTide.
Annotation order and aspect ordering
matter — measure once in a test that the
stack behaves the way you draw it on the
whiteboard.

8
00:01:17,352 --> 00:01:26,615
A common surprise: retry outside the
breaker retries into an open circuit; the
inverse ordering changes incident shape.
Walk symptoms.

9
00:01:26,736 --> 00:01:35,544
Without rate limiting, a berth storm
during fog season triggers vendor 429s and
then a connection storm when every
scheduler retries at once.

10
00:01:35,664 --> 00:01:42,984
With bulkhead alone, five tide calls run
and the sixth fails fast — scheduling UI
stays responsive for non-tide actions.

11
00:01:43,103 --> 00:01:57,144
Micrometer binds Resilience4j metrics
automatically when the dependency is
present: retry success/failure, bulkhead
rejected calls, breaker state. Those
series belong next to your gate timers in
the same scrape job.

12
00:01:57,263 --> 00:02:09,431
If resilience4j_bulkhead_rejected_calls
climbs while CPU is idle, you are
protecting the vendor correctly and may
need more capacity or a wider bulkhead — a
deliberate trade-off,

13
00:02:09,551 --> 00:02:20,039
not an automatic “raise the limit.” Trade-
offs: retries help idempotent GETs of tide
height; they are dangerous on non-
idempotent POSTs that book a berth side
effect.

14
00:02:20,159 --> 00:02:31,055
Semaphore bulkheads are light; thread-pool
bulkheads isolate better and cost threads.
Timeouts that are shorter than the
vendor’s p99 create self-inflicted failure
rates.

15
00:02:31,175 --> 00:02:39,095
Cached fallbacks keep the quay moving with
stale tide data — label them degraded so
operators do not treat them as truth for
deep-draft vessels.

16
00:02:39,215 --> 00:02:50,255
Name each Resilience4j instance after the
dependency (tideApi, billingQuote), not
after the calling class, so metrics and
runbooks stay portable when scheduling
refactor moves the call site.

17
00:02:50,375 --> 00:02:59,327
Shared names across unrelated vendors
accidentally couple their breaker state —
a tide outage should not open billing’s
breaker because someone reused
remoteCalls.

18
00:02:59,447 --> 00:03:12,287
A misconception is retrying non-idempotent
POSTs until you double-book berths —
retries need idempotent semantics or
idempotency keys. Another is unbounded
retries without jitter that amplify an
outage.

19
00:03:12,407 --> 00:03:23,951
A third is one global bulkhead for every
remote system so tide contention starves
billing calls that deserved isolation.
HTTP and resilience cover request/response
paths.

20
00:03:24,071 --> 00:03:32,855
Some harbor facts should not wait for a
synchronous call: a berth assignment
changed, and billing and the yard display
both need to know without gate
orchestrating them.

21
00:03:32,975 --> 00:03:36,695
That push model is messaging with Spring
Cloud Stream.
